- P-37 **One library, the interpreter's, serves both paths: the compiled path checks it, compiles it and runs it.** The interpreter's prelude, `FortressLibrary` and `FortressBuiltin`, becomes the compiled path's prelude in place of the compiler's own, and the compiled path reads the interpreter's library with it. The compiler's prelude, the files `CompilerLibrary`, `CompilerBuiltin` and `CompilerAlgebra`, is a bootstrap to remove: no declaration is added to it, and at the switch-over its three files are deleted and their tests kept (P-38 says which tests are respelled). Why, as the curator put it: the switch-over exists to avoid porting walk's library into the compiler's library, and the revival continues the type checker's development so that it accepts the fuller library that walk uses. The ground in the language (P-26 defines the layers): the specification names one set of default libraries, chiefly `FortressLibrary` and `FortressBuiltin`, and never names the compiler's. Beside the natives (P-33), the switch-over needs two zeros over the fourteen components, which are the distance stage's twelve and the two that `List` imports, `CovariantCollection` and `OneShotFlag`: the checker finds no error, and the code generator refuses no declaration. The decision assumes that the compiled path can run the library, not only check it. Measured: the code generator refuses 52 declarations of the twelve that the checker passes (F-34). Not measured: any component of the library compiled and run. So the premise is unmeasured, not refuted. P-33 says when the library's other components are cleared. The decision settles which files survive, not the designs inside them. The compiler's prelude belongs to the team's implementation on the JVM; the team called parts of it "bogus" and the switch to it a "hopefully temporary hack" (`WellKnownNames.java:113-116`); its designs are evidence, not authority. A prelude design that differs from the library's binds the one library in two cases only: the team's later word on the language backs it, as the exclusion rule backs the flat number types (P-43); or the language's text is silent or ambiguous and the team's compiled checker enforces the design as a rule (P-158). A prelude line counts as the team's later word only if it is later than the library's text on the same shape, by P-26's dating, and no later text of the language contradicts it. A stub does not bind, and neither does a workaround of a JVM or checker gap, such as fixed arities in place of varargs. This decision does not stop work on the code generator, which is compiler work under either library. The decision is not put to the curator again. How the checker comes to accept the library, its first step and the order of the work, is the plan that the curator approved (P-33).
